00:00:03,061 --> 00:00:04,835
Inside a growing thunderstorm,

3
00:00:05,072 --> 00:00:06,215
over southern Florida.

4
00:00:06,882 --> 00:00:08,432
A little over a minute later,

5
00:00:08,673 --> 00:00:11,322
radar shows this jet at eight thousand seven

6
00:00:11,342 --> 00:00:12,679
hundred and fifty feet.

7
00:00:13,116 --> 00:00:15,584
Falling faster than thirty thousand feet

8
00:00:15,605 --> 00:00:18,721
per minute. This is the physics of those

9
00:00:18,741 --> 00:00:19,633
sixty seconds.

10
00:00:23,026 --> 00:00:25,956
On November first, a Learjet 60 business

11
00:00:25,976 --> 00:00:27,823
jet left Austin, Texas,

12
00:00:27,996 --> 00:00:30,270
bound for North Eleuthera in the Bahamas.

13
00:00:30,857 --> 00:00:32,599
Just before one in the afternoon,

14
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:35,289
it came down in a residential neighborhood

15
00:00:35,309 --> 00:00:37,080
in Greenacres, Palm Beach County.

16
00:00:37,705 --> 00:00:39,872
All nine people on board were killed.

17
00:00:40,492 --> 00:00:42,792
The official cause has not been determined.

18
00:00:43,372 --> 00:00:45,884
But the flight data already tells a story,

19
00:00:46,391 --> 00:00:48,962
and it is a story about physics.

20
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:54,223
At around twelve forty-seven,

21
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:56,769
the crew told controllers they were picking

22
00:00:56,790 --> 00:00:57,848
up light clear ice,

23
00:00:58,083 --> 00:00:59,283
moderate turbulence,

24
00:00:59,303 --> 00:01:01,799
and altitude swings of about seven hundred

25
00:01:01,819 --> 00:01:04,822
feet. Weather radar suggests the jet had

26
00:01:04,842 --> 00:01:07,297
flown into a rapidly building thunderstorm

27
00:01:07,317 --> 00:01:09,861
cell. Inside a cumulonimbus,

28
00:01:10,092 --> 00:01:13,073
updrafts can exceed thirty meters per second.

29
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:15,896
They loft water droplets miles above the

30
00:01:15,917 --> 00:01:18,584
freezing level, where they stay liquid far

31
00:01:18,604 --> 00:01:20,644
below zero. Supercooled.

32
00:01:21,188 --> 00:01:22,497
The moment they hit a wing,

33
00:01:22,847 --> 00:01:23,646
they freeze.

34
00:01:27,104 --> 00:01:28,590
Here is why that matters.

35
00:01:29,136 --> 00:01:30,979
Lift equals one half,

36
00:01:31,247 --> 00:01:34,190
times air density, times velocity squared,

37
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:37,428
times wing area, times the lift coefficient.

38
00:01:38,024 --> 00:01:40,519
That coefficient depends on the exact shape

39
00:01:40,539 --> 00:01:43,606
of the wing. A thin ridge of ice on the

40
00:01:43,626 --> 00:01:45,823
leading edge trips the smooth airflow.

41
00:01:46,307 --> 00:01:48,941
Maximum lift can drop by more than thirty

42
00:01:48,961 --> 00:01:51,441
percent, and the wing stalls at a lower

43
00:01:51,461 --> 00:01:52,339
angle of attack.

44
00:01:55,773 --> 00:01:59,255
Now add altitude. At thirty-five thousand

45
00:01:59,275 --> 00:02:02,032
feet, the air has less than a third of its

46
00:02:02,052 --> 00:02:04,879
sea-level density. And because it is colder,

47
00:02:05,138 --> 00:02:06,909
the speed of sound is lower too.

48
00:02:07,367 --> 00:02:09,228
So the stall speed rises,

49
00:02:09,442 --> 00:02:10,736
while the maximum speed,

50
00:02:10,939 --> 00:02:12,682
set by Mach, falls.

51
00:02:13,174 --> 00:02:14,618
Between those two walls,

52
00:02:14,819 --> 00:02:16,376
the safe band narrows.

53
00:02:16,807 --> 00:02:19,051
Pilots call the point where they meet the

54
00:02:19,071 --> 00:02:22,109
coffin corner. Ice pushes the stall wall

55
00:02:22,129 --> 00:02:24,909
forward. Turbulence throws the aircraft

56
00:02:24,929 --> 00:02:28,086
back and forth. And the margin can vanish

57
00:02:28,106 --> 00:02:28,858
in seconds.

58
00:02:32,279 --> 00:02:34,725
The crew asked to climb to thirty-seven

59
00:02:34,745 --> 00:02:36,965
thousand feet. Moments later,

60
00:02:37,170 --> 00:02:40,075
they reported an overspeed and a rapid descent.

61
00:02:40,641 --> 00:02:42,984
Flight data shows the jet descending at

62
00:02:43,004 --> 00:02:45,581
more than thirty-two thousand feet per minute.

63
00:02:46,065 --> 00:02:48,561
That is over one hundred and sixty meters

64
00:02:48,581 --> 00:02:51,055
per second, more than three hundred and

65
00:02:51,076 --> 00:02:52,242
sixty miles per hour,

66
00:02:52,548 --> 00:02:55,495
straight down. Near the speed of sound,

67
00:02:55,730 --> 00:02:57,858
shock waves form on top of the wing.

68
00:02:58,333 --> 00:03:00,291
The center of lift shifts backward,

69
00:03:00,573 --> 00:03:03,190
and the nose wants to pitch down even further.

70
00:03:03,624 --> 00:03:05,159
It is called Mach tuck.

71
00:03:05,683 --> 00:03:08,091
And because aerodynamic loads grow with

72
00:03:08,112 --> 00:03:09,381
the square of speed,

73
00:03:09,724 --> 00:03:12,221
the structure can be pushed past its design

74
00:03:12,242 --> 00:03:15,378
limits. The NTSB says there is evidence

75
00:03:15,398 --> 00:03:17,797
the aircraft broke apart in flight.

76
00:03:21,272 --> 00:03:23,243
None of this is a conclusion yet.

77
00:03:23,798 --> 00:03:26,724
The NTSB is still recovering wreckage and

78
00:03:26,744 --> 00:03:29,696
data, and a preliminary report is expected

79
00:03:29,715 --> 00:03:32,638
within weeks. The probable cause may take

80
00:03:32,659 --> 00:03:35,799
more than a year. But the lesson is an old

81
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:38,733
one in aviation. You don't fly through a

82
00:03:38,754 --> 00:03:40,870
thunderstorm. You go around it,

83
00:03:41,201 --> 00:03:43,238
by twenty nautical miles or more.

84
00:03:43,792 --> 00:03:46,747
Because inside, physics doesn't negotiate.

85
00:03:47,569 --> 00:03:49,688
In memory of the nine lives lost.
